    "current_example": {
      "math_academy_id": "e-122",
      "problem": "Find the interval where the average rate of change of $f(x)$ equals $0$ given the following table of values for $f(x)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n| $x$ | $\\,-2\\,$ | $\\,-1\\,$ | $\\,0\\,$ | $\\,1\\,$ | $\\,2\\,$ | $\\,5\\,$ | $\\,7\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $\\,2\\,$ | $\\,1\\,$ | $\\,4\\,$ | $\\,-5\\,$ | $\\,-7\\,$ | $\\,2\\,$ | $\\,-2\\,$ |",
      "worked_solution": "We have $\\dfrac{\\Delta f}{\\Delta x}=0$ if and only if $\\Delta f = 0.$ So\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & Δf=0 \\\\  & f(b)-f(a)=0 \\\\  & f(b)=f(a).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means we have to find an interval $[a,b]$ such that $f(a)=f(b).$ \n\nSince $f(-2)=f(5)=2,$ we conclude that $\\dfrac{\\Delta f}{\\Delta x}=0$ on the interval $[-2,5].$",
